---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import BearingCalculator from '../components/BearingCalculator.astro';
import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';
import RelatedTools from '../components/RelatedTools.astro';
---

<Layout
	title="Bearing Calculator & Coordinate Converter Online — DD, DMS, UTM"
	description="Free online bearing calculator and universal coordinate format converter. Compute great-circle distance and forward azimuth between points. Convert bi-directionally between Decimal Degrees (DD), DMS, UTM, and Plus Codes."
	path="/bearing/"
	keywords="bearing calculator, coordinate converter, dd to dms, utm converter, great circle distance, forward azimuth, plus code converter, gps coordinates conversion"
>
	<PageHero
		eyebrow="bearing calculator · coordinate converter"
		title="Plot courses and convert coordinates with ease."
		description="Compute the initial and final bearing, great-circle distance, and visual directional course between any two points. Convert seamlessly between Decimal Degrees (DD), Degrees Minutes Seconds (DMS), UTM, and Google Plus Codes."
	>
		<BearingCalculator />
	</PageHero>

	<section class="border-t border-hairline bg-canvas">
		<div class="mx-auto max-w-[1200px] grid gap-6 px-4 py-16 sm:px-6 md:grid-cols-3">
			<div>
				<p class="eyebrow mb-2">geodesic navigation</p>
				<p class="text-sm text-body leading-6">
					Calculates the true great-circle course vector and distance with the Haversine spherical formula, including both initial departure heading and final arrival back azimuth.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div>
				<p class="eyebrow mb-2">universal coordinate converter</p>
				<p class="text-sm text-body leading-6">
					Zero external API dependencies. Converts instantly between Decimal Degrees (DD), traditional Degrees Minutes Seconds (DMS), military UTM grid zones, and Open Location Codes.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div>
				<p class="eyebrow mb-2">departure telemetry</p>
				<p class="text-sm text-body leading-6">
					Provides instant local navigation telemetry from your point of origin, including local magnetic declination, Qibla alignment, and solar compass position.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="border-t border-hairline bg-canvas">
		<div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-20 sm:px-6 md:py-24">
			<p class="eyebrow">geodetic principles & navigation math</p>
			<h2 class="mt-3 text-display-lg text-ink">Great-Circle Bearings and Coordinate Systems Explained</h2>

			<p class="mt-6 text-body-lg text-body">
				Navigating across Earth's curved surface requires understanding two fundamental disciplines: <strong class="text-ink font-medium">geodetic coordinate representation</strong> and <strong class="text-ink font-medium">spherical trigonometry</strong>. This dual-purpose utility combines an industrial-grade Great-Circle Bearing Calculator with a Universal Coordinate Format Converter, allowing you to plot courses, convert map coordinates, and calculate geodesic vectors without installing specialized GIS software.
			</p>
			<p class="mt-4 leading-7">
				A <strong class="text-ink font-medium">bearing</strong> is the horizontal angle measured clockwise from True North (0°) to a destination waypoint: 90° corresponds to East, 180° to South, and 270° to West. For example, departing from London toward New York requires setting an initial compass course of approximately 288° (WNW).
			</p>

			<h3 class="mt-10 text-display-sm text-ink">Initial Bearing vs. Final Bearing (Back Azimuth)</h3>
			<p class="mt-3 leading-7">
				On a flat Mercator projection map, a straight line between two points represents a <strong class="text-ink font-medium">rhumb line</strong> (loxodrome) — a path of constant compass heading. However, because Earth is an oblate sphere, the shortest geometric path over the surface is a <strong class="text-ink font-medium">great-circle route</strong> (orthodrome).
			</p>
			<p class="mt-4 leading-7">
				Along a great-circle route, the true compass heading changes continuously at every mile of travel. For this reason, marine navigators and airline pilots distinguish between two key vectors:
			</p>
			<ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 pl-5 list-disc text-body text-sm leading-6">
				<li>
					<strong class="text-ink">Initial Bearing (&alpha;₁):</strong> The forward azimuth at the moment of departure. This is the heading you steer when setting out from Point A. It is derived using spherical trigonometry:
					<div class="my-3 p-3 rounded-lg bg-canvas-soft border border-hairline font-mono text-xs text-ink overflow-x-auto text-center">
						&alpha;₁ = atan2( sin(&Delta;&lambda;) &times; cos(&phi;₂), cos(&phi;₁) &times; sin(&phi;₂) - sin(&phi;₁) &times; cos(&phi;₂) &times; cos(&Delta;&lambda;) )
					</div>
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong class="text-ink">Final Bearing (&alpha;₂):</strong> The heading of the great circle as it arrives at Point B. For transoceanic voyages, &alpha;₂ can differ from &alpha;₁ by tens of degrees. For example, a flight from London to New York begins at 288° and arrives at 231° (SW).
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong class="text-ink">Back Azimuth:</strong> The exact reverse heading pointing from Point B back toward Point A, calculated as <span class="font-mono text-xs">(Initial Bearing + 180°) mod 360°</span>.
				</li>
			</ul>

			<h3 class="mt-10 text-display-sm text-ink">Universal Coordinate Formats: DD, DMS, UTM, and Plus Codes</h3>
			<p class="mt-3 leading-7">
				Different mapping disciplines and industries employ distinct coordinate notation systems. Our built-in format converter translates bi-directionally across the four most prominent standards:
			</p>

			<div class="mt-4 space-y-4 text-sm text-body">
				<div class="card-soft p-4">
					<h4 class="font-semibold text-ink text-base">1. Decimal Degrees (DD) — e.g. 40.712800°, -74.006000°</h4>
					<p class="mt-1 leading-6">
						The modern digital standard used by web map APIs (Google Maps, Leaflet, Mapbox) and GPS receivers. North and East are represented by positive values; South and West are represented by negative values. Six decimal places provide pinpoint resolution down to approximately 11 centimeters.
					</p>
				</div>

				<div class="card-soft p-4">
					<h4 class="font-semibold text-ink text-base">2. Degrees Minutes Seconds (DMS) — e.g. 40° 42' 46.1" N, 74° 0' 21.6" W</h4>
					<p class="mt-1 leading-6">
						The traditional astronomical and maritime format dating back to ancient sexagesimal geometry. One degree equals 60 arcminutes (1 minute of latitude ≈ 1 nautical mile ≈ 1.852 km); one arcminute equals 60 arcseconds (1 second ≈ 30.8 meters). Widely used in nautical charts, military firing tables, and topographical land deeds.
					</p>
				</div>

				<div class="card-soft p-4">
					<h4 class="font-semibold text-ink text-base">3. Universal Transverse Mercator (UTM) — e.g. 18T 583959m E 4507351m N</h4>
					<p class="mt-1 leading-6">
						A metric grid projection that divides Earth into 60 longitudinal zones, each 6 degrees wide, using the WGS84 datum. Positions are expressed as linear meters of <em>Easting</em> (relative to a 500,000m central meridian) and <em>Northing</em> (distance from the equator). Because UTM uses standard meters rather than angular degrees, it is the primary standard for search and rescue (SAR), wilderness orienteering, forestry, and military tactical operations.
					</p>
				</div>

				<div class="card-soft p-4">
					<h4 class="font-semibold text-ink text-base">4. Google Plus Codes (Open Location Code / OLC) — e.g. 87G8P2X7+9P</h4>
					<p class="mt-1 leading-6">
						A free, open-source geocoding system developed by Google that converts latitude and longitude into concise alphanumeric strings. Plus Codes function as digital street addresses for rural settlements, remote islands, hiking trails, and disaster relief zones lacking conventional postal addresses.
					</p>
				</div>
			</div>

			<!-- Comprehensive FAQ Section -->
			<h3 class="mt-14 text-display-sm text-ink">Frequently Asked Questions</h3>
			<div class="mt-6 space-y-6 text-sm divide-y divide-hairline">
				<div class="pt-6 first:pt-0">
					<h4 class="font-semibold text-ink text-base">How do I convert between Decimal Degrees and DMS manually?</h4>
					<p class="mt-2 text-body leading-6">
						To convert DMS to Decimal: divide seconds by 3,600, divide minutes by 60, and add them to the whole degrees: <span class="font-mono text-xs">Degrees + (Minutes / 60) + (Seconds / 3600)</span>. For South latitude or West longitude, make the resulting number negative. To convert Decimal to DMS: the whole number is the degree; multiply the remaining decimal fraction by 60 to get minutes; multiply that remaining fraction by 60 to get seconds. Our online converter automates this instantly.
					</p>
				</div>

				<div class="pt-6">
					<h4 class="font-semibold text-ink text-base">What is the difference between Haversine distance and map distance?</h4>
					<p class="mt-2 text-body leading-6">
						Measuring distance with a ruler on a flat paper map introduces severe scale distortions (the Greenland problem on Mercator projections). The <strong class="text-ink font-medium">Haversine formula</strong> applies spherical trigonometry across Earth's mean volumetric radius (6,371.0088 km) to compute true great-circle surface distance, matching real-world airline flight miles.
					</p>
				</div>

				<div class="pt-6">
					<h4 class="font-semibold text-ink text-base">How do I steer with the calculated bearing using my compass?</h4>
					<p class="mt-2 text-body leading-6">
						Calculate the initial bearing between your departure point and destination. Next, open our <a href="/" class="text-link underline">online digital compass</a>. If your compass is set to True North, rotate yourself until the red lubber heading indicator matches your calculated bearing number. If steering with a magnetic compass, apply the magnetic declination offset displayed in the telemetry panel.
					</p>
				</div>

				<div class="pt-6">
					<h4 class="font-semibold text-ink text-base">Are coordinate conversions performed on a server?</h4>
					<p class="mt-2 text-body leading-6">
						No. All mathematical transformations — including the Redfearn series equations for UTM forward and inverse projection, Plus Code base-20 encoding, and spherical geodesics — execute 100% client-side in your browser's JavaScript engine. Your coordinates are never sent across the internet, ensuring total privacy.
					</p>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<RelatedTools current="/bearing/" />
</Layout>
